  "story": {
    "headline": "Sad or afraid? Dogs may be able to read your face",
    "summary": "A new brain-imaging study suggests that dogs may be able to distinguish between certain negative human emotions just by looking at people’s faces.",
    "publisher": "Rally News",
    "url": "https://rally.news/categories/ai/?article=Sad%2Bor%2Bafraid%3F%2BDogs%2Bmay%2Bbe%2Bable%2Bto%2Bread%2Byour%2Bface"
  },
  "researchBrief": "The study, published in the journal iScience on August 7, 2026, involved 12 dogs from loving families. Researchers used functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) to scan the dogs' brains while showing them images of human faces expressing happiness, sadness, anger, and fear. The study found that dogs' brains showed distinct activity patterns for different emotions, particularly happiness. The temporal cortex-caudate nucleus network responded uniquely to happiness, allowing researchers to distinguish brain reactions to smiles from all three negative expressions. The study also provided the first evidence that dogs can distinguish between specific negative facial expressions, including anger, sadness, and fear. The researchers noted that this ability does not mean dogs experience emotions exactly as humans do. The study was corroborated by multiple sources, including Scientific American, Phys.org, Time, and EurekAlert!",
  "sources": [
    "https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/can-dogs-read-your-facial-expressions-it-depends-on-your-mood/",
    "https://phys.org/news/2026-08-mri-dogs-distinguish-human-sadness.html",
    "https://time.com/article/2026/08/10/your-dog-can-tell-when-youre-happy/",
    "https://e3.eurekalert.org/news-releases/1137798"
  ],
